[SPEAKER_00]: And it's pretty difficult to take a fellow and put him in the Hall of Fame when they bounce the ball and the catcher stood way the hell behind the bat and all this kind of thing.

557
00:48:29,620 --> 00:48:37,704
[SPEAKER_00]: And foul, counter the third strike, and all this business.

558
00:48:41,066 --> 00:48:46,028
[SPEAKER_00]: My belief that organized baseball started in 1900 or 1901.

559
00:48:46,468 --> 00:48:48,869
[SPEAKER_00]: There should be a line of demarcation there too.

560
00:48:49,369 --> 00:49:00,453
[SPEAKER_00]: Beyond that, back beyond that, should be old-timers and they should be classified such and they should be a special standard and there should be a special room with these fellows in it.

561
00:49:05,707 --> 00:49:17,717
[SPEAKER_00]: today playing 161 games, 162 games, when you get down to the thing, there's a hell of a lot of difference in what's taking place today and what took place in our day.

562
00:49:19,399 --> 00:49:26,124
[SPEAKER_00]: I very often, I refuse to make comparisons because there's no way to make comparisons.

563
00:49:26,164 --> 00:49:28,827
[SPEAKER_00]: They make a great deal of strikeouts today.

564
00:49:29,487 --> 00:49:32,730
[SPEAKER_00]: Strikeouts didn't mean anything to us.

565
00:49:32,750 --> 00:49:33,411
[SPEAKER_00]: Ridiculous.

566
00:49:34,435 --> 00:49:38,196
[SPEAKER_02]: There's a couple of things in your own career I wanted to check on.

567
00:49:39,137 --> 00:49:41,538
[SPEAKER_02]: You came in just about the time the spitball was outlawed.

568
00:49:42,598 --> 00:49:46,440
[SPEAKER_00]: It was outlawed around 1921.

569
00:49:47,880 --> 00:49:49,101
[SPEAKER_02]: Had you thrown it before then?

570
00:49:49,121 --> 00:49:50,821
[SPEAKER_02]: No, I never threw the spitball.

571
00:49:50,841 --> 00:49:53,102
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever think about it before it was outlawed?

572
00:49:53,122 --> 00:49:54,403
[SPEAKER_00]: No, neither did anybody else.

573
00:49:55,563 --> 00:49:58,144
[SPEAKER_00]: There's a point, if you're writing anything, make a point of it.

574
00:49:58,694 --> 00:49:59,494
[SPEAKER_02]: Well, why is that?

575
00:49:59,514 --> 00:50:02,656
[SPEAKER_02]: Why would some throw it and a few throw it and others wouldn't?

576
00:50:02,676 --> 00:50:03,937
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, we didn't think anything of it.

577
00:50:03,957 --> 00:50:06,198
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't think it any better than our curve.

578
00:50:07,018 --> 00:50:10,300
[SPEAKER_01]: That wasn't that effective.

579
00:50:10,700 --> 00:50:18,804
[SPEAKER_00]: Mainly the spitball pitches were effective, but there were a hell of a lot of guys that threw the spitball that weren't effective, too.

580
00:50:22,353 --> 00:50:24,935
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't think that the spitball was unusual.

581
00:50:25,335 --> 00:50:28,617
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, we thought the stuff we had was just as good as that.

582
00:50:29,738 --> 00:50:32,340
[SPEAKER_02]: How about other foreign substance, emery?

583
00:50:33,021 --> 00:50:39,625
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, that was barred when I was in the Army.

584
00:50:39,685 --> 00:50:42,127
[SPEAKER_00]: Dave Danforth and those guys, they did that.

585
00:50:42,147 --> 00:50:43,708
[SPEAKER_00]: What did Danforth do with that ball?

586
00:50:44,329 --> 00:50:48,051
[SPEAKER_02]: He got in all kinds of trouble with Dan Johnson.

587
00:50:48,091 --> 00:50:49,472
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know what he did with it.

588
00:50:49,512 --> 00:50:51,734
[SPEAKER_00]: He had some kind of a roof.

589
00:50:52,788 --> 00:50:56,329
[SPEAKER_00]: either a rough fingernail or a rough something or other.

590
00:50:56,349 --> 00:50:58,770
[SPEAKER_00]: He roughed up one side of the ball.

591
00:50:58,850 --> 00:51:07,653
[SPEAKER_00]: And Billy Evans saved 13 balls one time against us in St. Louis.

592
00:51:07,673 --> 00:51:13,255
[SPEAKER_00]: And those were the balls they sent him to Ben Johnson and the league.

593
00:51:13,275 --> 00:51:15,596
[SPEAKER_00]: And that's what cost him his job.

594
00:51:17,656 --> 00:51:18,297
[SPEAKER_00]: Whose job?

595
00:51:18,837 --> 00:51:19,457
[SPEAKER_00]: Danforth.

596
00:51:21,888 --> 00:51:26,509
[SPEAKER_02]: Bill, you haven't saved the ball Danforth was throwing to show how he had scarred it.

597
00:51:27,529 --> 00:51:28,769
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, I can now.

598
00:51:28,789 --> 00:51:30,090
[SPEAKER_00]: See, now you're going overboard.

599
00:51:30,150 --> 00:51:33,390
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know whether they scarred it or shined it.

600
00:51:33,910 --> 00:51:47,173
[SPEAKER_00]: Now Urban Shocker used to use a spitball pitch, yeah, and he would suck the seams and wet them and suck them and then he

601
00:51:51,249 --> 00:51:58,574
[SPEAKER_00]: He'd get dirt on him, or tobacco, and press them with his finger behind his glove.

602
00:52:00,215 --> 00:52:06,098
[SPEAKER_00]: He could raise the seam a little bit, see, and all you had to do was raise the seam a little bit and you could make the ball sail like hell.

603
00:52:06,899 --> 00:52:16,525
[SPEAKER_00]: And a lot of these fellas had little tricks that they could employ once in a while if you could catch them at it.

604
00:52:19,206 --> 00:52:27,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Dan Forth was supposed to pitch against Waller Johnson in an exhibition game after the season was over in Baltimore at one time.

605
00:52:28,332 --> 00:52:41,600
[SPEAKER_00]: And it was two all-star teams, like George Sissel played first base, Eddie Collins second base, and the one around there, that caliber of player playing, and the two teams of that nature going to play each other for some charity.

606
00:52:41,640 --> 00:52:43,801
[SPEAKER_00]: And Johnson was going to pitch against Dan Forth.

607
00:52:50,431 --> 00:53:12,445
[SPEAKER_00]: Johnson went over to the other ball club and said, look, he said, I'm going to have to tell you, Johnson was a very quiet fellow, went over and said, look, he said, I'm going to have to tell you fellows that you better be a little bit loose up at the plate because if any of those balls sail and anybody gets hit.

608
00:53:12,805 --> 00:53:13,506
[SPEAKER_00]: By Danforth?

609
00:53:14,066 --> 00:53:15,567
[SPEAKER_00]: No, by Johnson.

610
00:53:16,428 --> 00:53:18,269
[SPEAKER_00]: But the balls would, if they,

611
00:53:19,245 --> 00:53:24,406
[SPEAKER_00]: You see that they were hung over until the next inning and Johnson pitched them as fast as he was.

612
00:53:24,886 --> 00:53:28,247
[SPEAKER_00]: If he hit anybody, it's not going to be my fault.

613
00:53:30,928 --> 00:53:38,790
[SPEAKER_02]: They had a lot of... Johnson, I guess, never went backwards, not to throw at people.

614
00:53:38,810 --> 00:53:41,691
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, he was a very... Did you bat against him?

615
00:53:41,771 --> 00:53:42,191
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah.

616
00:53:42,431 --> 00:53:45,111
[SPEAKER_00]: How did you like to bat against Walter Johnson?

617
00:53:45,131 --> 00:53:46,612
[SPEAKER_00]: We didn't do much batting against him.

618
00:53:46,632 --> 00:53:47,512
[SPEAKER_00]: We just stood there.

619
00:53:48,298 --> 00:53:50,139
[SPEAKER_00]: At least I did.

620
00:53:50,159 --> 00:53:51,220
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever hit him?

621
00:53:51,620 --> 00:53:52,781
[SPEAKER_00]: Or hit the ball?

622
00:53:52,921 --> 00:53:54,482
[SPEAKER_00]: Oh, hit the ball, yeah.

623
00:53:54,522 --> 00:53:56,944
[SPEAKER_00]: But I never got any hits on him, I recall.

624
00:53:57,725 --> 00:54:01,107
[SPEAKER_00]: But he was wicked.

625
00:54:01,227 --> 00:54:03,389
[SPEAKER_00]: He was wicked in the way he pitched sidearm.

626
00:54:05,490 --> 00:54:06,991
[SPEAKER_00]: I'd like to see him pitching today.

627
00:54:07,332 --> 00:54:07,792
[SPEAKER_02]: Wow.

628
00:54:08,032 --> 00:54:10,154
[SPEAKER_02]: He was probably the fastest pitcher you've ever seen.

629
00:54:10,174 --> 00:54:12,695
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, in my opinion, the fastest I've ever pitched.

630
00:54:12,715 --> 00:54:14,376
[SPEAKER_00]: These fellas don't know why.

631
00:54:14,437 --> 00:54:16,118
[SPEAKER_00]: And he had probably pretty good control.

632
00:54:16,639 --> 00:54:21,585
[SPEAKER_00]: They take a fellow like, now this is a boast, and I admit it's a boast.

633
00:54:22,706 --> 00:54:27,331
[SPEAKER_00]: People, if they ever heard this or people do hear it, can make little of it if they want to.

634
00:54:27,712 --> 00:54:32,878
[SPEAKER_00]: But I'm telling you, and I'm not boasting, I mean I'm not being...

635
00:54:34,273 --> 00:54:43,536
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm a smart aleck when I say this, but I was fast myself, and I believe I was as fast as Seaver easily.

636
00:54:44,437 --> 00:54:51,339
[SPEAKER_00]: And I never struck out anybody, three a game, I think my average, something like that.

637
00:54:52,320 --> 00:54:53,820
[SPEAKER_00]: But that was neither here nor there.

638
00:54:53,880 --> 00:55:01,483
[SPEAKER_00]: We had on the 23 staff like Jones and Bush and Shockey and Mays, and Pennock wasn't fast,

639
00:55:03,038 --> 00:55:07,565
[SPEAKER_00]: But we had fellows on that staff, all of them could throw as fast as I could.

640
00:55:07,985 --> 00:55:13,173
[SPEAKER_00]: So there wasn't this business about being fast.

641
00:55:13,914 --> 00:55:17,499
[SPEAKER_00]: When we hear these fellows talk today about Nolan Ryan and

642
00:55:19,677 --> 00:55:23,958
[SPEAKER_00]: and Tom Seaver and these different guys, and Bob Gibson.

643
00:55:24,499 --> 00:55:25,039
[SPEAKER_00]: What the hell?

644
00:55:25,079 --> 00:55:28,540
[SPEAKER_00]: We had three and four guys in one club with that fast.

645
00:55:29,240 --> 00:55:32,341
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, we were, in those days, it was a different type of pitching.

646
00:55:32,421 --> 00:55:38,143
[SPEAKER_00]: It was fastball and curb, although they did throw knuckleballs and they did throw forkballs.

647
00:55:38,183 --> 00:55:41,304
[SPEAKER_00]: Joe Bush threw the forkball.

648
00:55:43,225 --> 00:55:49,227
[SPEAKER_00]: It always makes me laugh when you hear Williams and DiMaggio and these fellas talk and say that the pitchers didn't

649
00:55:50,475 --> 00:55:52,336
[SPEAKER_00]: only had fastballs and curves.

650
00:55:52,857 --> 00:55:56,640
[SPEAKER_00]: Hell, we had more stuff and different kinds of stuff than these fellas have today.

651
00:55:57,401 --> 00:56:02,225
[SPEAKER_00]: And on our ball club alone, I threw the palm ball, I'll show you a picture of it.

652
00:56:03,166 --> 00:56:11,114
[SPEAKER_00]: The palm ball, a three-quarter speed ball, the fastball, and the curve ball.

653
00:56:11,194 --> 00:56:13,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And the curve ball wasn't too good, I must admit.

654
00:56:14,477 --> 00:56:26,285
[SPEAKER_00]: But Joe Bush threw the fourth ball, Sam Jones threw a fadeaway and a curveball, Schalke threw two curves, and he's fastballing a change of pace.

655
00:56:28,517 --> 00:56:33,720
[SPEAKER_00]: Pennock had the overhand curve, the sidearm curve, and one of the best screwballs in the business.

656
00:56:34,461 --> 00:56:39,064
[SPEAKER_00]: And as I told you, and Shocker threw the spitball.

657
00:56:39,684 --> 00:56:43,407
[SPEAKER_00]: And where the hell are they?

658
00:56:43,467 --> 00:56:46,549
[SPEAKER_00]: And when you get down to it, what are they talking about?

659
00:56:48,070 --> 00:56:53,274
[SPEAKER_00]: I threw the slider toward the end of my career, and the slider is nothing new.

660
00:56:55,074 --> 00:56:58,276
[SPEAKER_00]: In fact, George Yulee invented the slider in Cleveland.

661
00:56:58,916 --> 00:57:02,258
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you ask these fellas today who George Yulee was, none of them know.

662
00:57:04,159 --> 00:57:06,280
[SPEAKER_02]: I talked to George Yulee in a real nice interview.

663
00:57:07,161 --> 00:57:09,142
[SPEAKER_02]: He had arm trouble every other year, it seemed like.

664
00:57:10,122 --> 00:57:11,163
[SPEAKER_02]: But didn't your arm get tired?

665
00:57:12,284 --> 00:57:12,644
[SPEAKER_02]: No.

666
00:57:12,724 --> 00:57:13,764
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you ever have arm trouble?

667
00:57:14,705 --> 00:57:17,687
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, yes, but not from throwing or pitching in a game.

668
00:57:17,767 --> 00:57:19,968
[SPEAKER_00]: I got arm trouble from throwing

669
00:57:20,612 --> 00:57:23,213
[SPEAKER_00]: 10 cent balls that had milk bottles in them.

670
00:57:23,293 --> 00:57:29,354
[SPEAKER_00]: I had one night in Philadelphia out at Bob, when Bob Carpenter, the owner of the Phillies, was just a little boy.

671
00:57:30,234 --> 00:57:36,955
[SPEAKER_00]: And Pennock took Babe and myself and Dugan out to this street fair out in Kennett Square.

672
00:57:36,995 --> 00:57:38,056
[SPEAKER_00]: That's where Pennock lived.

673
00:57:38,116 --> 00:57:42,076
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, and well, that's where the Carpenters lived, too, out there, near there.

674
00:57:42,676 --> 00:57:45,297
[SPEAKER_00]: And this was for Bob Carpenter's street fair out there.

675
00:57:45,317 --> 00:57:50,058
[SPEAKER_00]: And we stood off and threw these balls at this paper mache milk bottles, and I hurt my arm.

676
00:57:50,893 --> 00:58:00,055
[SPEAKER_00]: and swelled way up when I was out for six weeks, five weeks in 1926, and I might have won some more games.

677
00:58:00,415 --> 00:58:01,776
[SPEAKER_02]: What do you think caused it?

678
00:58:01,896 --> 00:58:03,316
[SPEAKER_02]: An unnatural motion?

679
00:58:03,856 --> 00:58:10,158
[SPEAKER_00]: Well, a ten-cent ball and not a big shoe or something up near my elbow.

680
00:58:10,698 --> 00:58:13,018
[SPEAKER_01]: What did Huggins say about that?

681
00:58:13,038 --> 00:58:13,939
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't know.

682
00:58:14,079 --> 00:58:15,299
[SPEAKER_00]: He thought I hurt it in the game.

683
00:58:16,859 --> 00:58:18,420
[SPEAKER_00]: He didn't disabuse him.

684
00:58:18,460 --> 00:58:18,760
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

685
00:58:22,736 --> 00:58:23,757
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah, but they...

686
00:58:25,878 --> 00:58:34,483
[SPEAKER_02]: I would think throwing a fastball pitcher, throwing it all so much, would begin to take some toll, but apparently... Fastball pitcher, no.

687
00:58:34,583 --> 00:58:39,866
[SPEAKER_00]: You can't, unless you twist your arm, throwing a fastball.

688
00:58:39,886 --> 00:58:45,669
[SPEAKER_00]: A fastball should be thrown, is released away from you, out from you.

689
00:58:46,469 --> 00:58:47,690
[SPEAKER_00]: Your arm comes forward,

690
00:58:48,221 --> 00:58:52,493
[SPEAKER_00]: and the ball is released as far as your step almost.

691
00:58:53,823 --> 00:58:55,084
[SPEAKER_00]: before you let the ball go.

692
00:58:55,104 --> 00:58:57,926
[SPEAKER_00]: A curveball, the elbow is brought in toward the side.

693
00:58:59,847 --> 00:59:06,612
[SPEAKER_00]: You pull it down, you pull your elbow down in toward your side and snap your wrist as you release it.

694
00:59:06,632 --> 00:59:15,718
[SPEAKER_00]: Really fastball pitchers are less likely to have... That's right, much less likely to have arm trouble than a curveball pitcher or a screwball pitcher.

695
00:59:19,174 --> 00:59:22,256
[SPEAKER_00]: But it amazes me, it amazes me.

696
00:59:22,616 --> 00:59:44,390
[SPEAKER_00]: And I say these things not as, please don't misunderstand, I say these things not as for personal aggrandizement in the image sense, but in 1936, when I was down there through, I think it was 36,

697
00:59:49,170 --> 00:59:50,051
[SPEAKER_00]: I was in Montclair.

698
00:59:50,071 --> 00:59:54,518
[SPEAKER_00]: Montclair, New Jersey was my home at the time, and Pittsburgh happened to be playing in Brooklyn.

699
00:59:55,720 --> 01:00:01,688
[SPEAKER_00]: And I was taken with appendicitis and carted off to the hospital and operated on.

700
01:00:06,022 --> 01:00:20,834
[SPEAKER_00]: Within four weeks, I finished a game in Chicago, and three days after pitching in Chicago for the workout, I pitched a 15-inning ball game against Freddie Fitzsimmons, and he beat me 2-1 in Pittsburgh.

701
01:00:20,894 --> 01:00:26,899
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, we had the bases loaded in the ninth inning with no one out and didn't score, and he beat me 2-1 in the 15th inning.

702
01:00:31,810 --> 01:00:35,272
[SPEAKER_00]: These guys today, they find it hard to pitch five or six innings.

703
01:00:35,673 --> 01:00:36,273
[SPEAKER_00]: Now, why?

704
01:00:36,413 --> 01:00:37,674
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

705
01:00:37,794 --> 01:00:48,021
[SPEAKER_00]: Look for me to the answer, because these are the things that I refuse to make comparisons, because I do not know why these things are taking place today.

706
01:00:48,061 --> 01:01:00,770
[SPEAKER_00]: They're either not trained to pitch these innings, or their philosophy is entirely different, because they are following the philosophy of the young people of the world today, or they're following the philosophy of labor.

707
01:01:01,928 --> 01:01:17,440
[SPEAKER_00]: and doing specialization jobs or just with time limits or being paid like a form of piecework or some damn thing, but it's not the philosophy that was used by the players of my day and

708
01:01:19,180 --> 01:01:29,208
[SPEAKER_00]: nor the work schedules and philosophies of your father or my father who poured in a full day and used to come struggling home and get up and go back to work the next day.

709
01:01:29,568 --> 01:01:35,433
[SPEAKER_00]: Now a lot of these fellows that work in factories get up and they go to work and they do the same thing.

710
01:01:35,453 --> 01:01:40,276
[SPEAKER_00]: They come home and they do whatever they do and they go back and work in the factory again.

711
01:01:40,336 --> 01:01:47,722
[SPEAKER_00]: But they're getting so many fringe benefits and so much time off, their working conditions are entirely different.

712
01:01:48,271 --> 01:01:52,593
[SPEAKER_00]: So the comparisons are a little bit stupid, I think.

713
01:01:53,694 --> 01:02:01,177
[SPEAKER_02]: When you came up with the Red Sox, and even after you were over the Yankees, did any of the older players or pitchers or coaches give you any help?

714
01:02:01,217 --> 01:02:02,178
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no.

715
01:02:02,498 --> 01:02:03,639
[SPEAKER_02]: You were on your own.

716
01:02:03,699 --> 01:02:06,160
[SPEAKER_00]: The only fellow that ever helped me was Herb Pennock.

717
01:02:07,400 --> 01:02:10,302
[SPEAKER_00]: And that was just a matter of personal friendship.

718
01:02:10,342 --> 01:02:14,144
[SPEAKER_00]: That's why I laugh at this Carl Mays thing.

719
01:02:14,204 --> 01:02:16,245
[SPEAKER_00]: The only guy that ever helped me was Pennock.

720
01:02:17,948 --> 01:02:41,116
[SPEAKER_00]: We had a long conversation on the train one night and he explained what he thought I was doing wrong and it was a matter of pitching routine and to the point where the next year I developed almost a catechism about pitching.

721
01:02:46,316 --> 01:02:49,419
[SPEAKER_00]: It worked.

722
01:02:49,799 --> 01:02:53,943
[SPEAKER_00]: He was more corrective in judgment.

723
01:02:54,163 --> 01:02:55,224
[SPEAKER_00]: There was another thing.

724
01:02:57,106 --> 01:03:00,829
[SPEAKER_00]: We talked about pitching and playing.

725
01:03:01,089 --> 01:03:01,770
[SPEAKER_00]: I don't know.

726
01:03:01,990 --> 01:03:02,931
[SPEAKER_00]: I can't tell you.

727
01:03:03,591 --> 01:03:07,735
[SPEAKER_00]: You see, now I was about to make a statement, and I was about to make a statement which would be

728
01:03:11,235 --> 01:03:17,678
[SPEAKER_00]: But you couldn't vouch for its veracity because I don't know.

729
01:03:17,738 --> 01:03:19,018
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not around these fellas.

730
01:03:19,079 --> 01:03:20,559
[SPEAKER_00]: So I don't travel with them.

731
01:03:20,859 --> 01:03:22,500
[SPEAKER_00]: I'm not in their conversations.

732
01:03:22,940 --> 01:03:23,701
[SPEAKER_00]: So how would I know?

733
01:03:24,561 --> 01:03:25,661
[SPEAKER_00]: It doesn't strike me.

734
01:03:26,002 --> 01:03:27,502
[SPEAKER_00]: It's an impression of mine.

735
01:03:27,802 --> 01:03:28,743
[SPEAKER_00]: Let's put it this way.

736
01:03:29,263 --> 01:03:33,905
[SPEAKER_00]: That they don't talk about pitching the way or playing the way we did.

737
01:03:33,965 --> 01:03:34,885
[SPEAKER_00]: Joe Morgan does.

738
01:03:36,166 --> 01:03:37,887
[SPEAKER_00]: The Reds and Rose does.

739
01:03:39,308 --> 01:03:39,788
[SPEAKER_00]: I know that.

740
01:03:41,590 --> 01:03:46,993
