1124
01:33:10,032 --> 01:33:11,412
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's a very nice label.

1125
01:33:12,712 --> 01:33:14,793
[SPEAKER_01]: I've been called almost everything.

1126
01:33:17,413 --> 01:33:21,736
[SPEAKER_02]: That struck me as very appropriate from what I've seen and read about you.

1127
01:33:22,257 --> 01:33:24,038
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you hear it very often?

1128
01:33:25,279 --> 01:33:26,580
[SPEAKER_02]: Are you familiar with that term?

1129
01:33:26,640 --> 01:33:29,162
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, somewhat.

1130
01:33:29,643 --> 01:33:30,343
[SPEAKER_00]: Who did he say?

1131
01:33:30,603 --> 01:33:31,524
[SPEAKER_00]: Will Wedge called it?

1132
01:33:31,544 --> 01:33:32,565
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, the New York Sun.

1133
01:33:32,725 --> 01:33:40,992
[SPEAKER_01]: And also, if you'll pardon me,

1134
01:33:46,040 --> 01:33:49,502
[SPEAKER_01]: He just died, the communist for the New York Times.

1135
01:33:52,764 --> 01:33:53,184
[SPEAKER_01]: Daly?

1136
01:33:53,485 --> 01:33:54,545
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Arthur Daly.

1137
01:33:55,126 --> 01:33:58,768
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, Arthur Daly.

1138
01:34:01,069 --> 01:34:02,570
[SPEAKER_01]: He was a good friend of mine, too.

1139
01:34:03,270 --> 01:34:06,853
[SPEAKER_01]: You've got to be a friend of those fellas to have them call you that.

1140
01:34:06,873 --> 01:34:10,335
[SPEAKER_01]: You had good relations with the press then, huh?

1141
01:34:10,435 --> 01:34:13,877
[SPEAKER_01]: Very, very, very good relations, yes.

1142
01:34:13,957 --> 01:34:14,197
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

1143
01:34:17,117 --> 01:34:22,119
[SPEAKER_01]: Frankie Graham was one of my finest friends, Frankie Graham.

1144
01:34:22,799 --> 01:34:24,379
[SPEAKER_01]: He wrote the history of the Yankees?

1145
01:34:25,279 --> 01:34:25,740
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1146
01:34:25,780 --> 01:34:28,060
[SPEAKER_02]: Did you read that?

1147
01:34:28,080 --> 01:34:29,881
[SPEAKER_00]: The old history, I wrote part of it.

1148
01:34:31,121 --> 01:34:31,461
[SPEAKER_02]: You what?

1149
01:34:31,681 --> 01:34:32,541
[SPEAKER_01]: I wrote part of it.

1150
01:34:32,561 --> 01:34:33,082
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

1151
01:34:33,242 --> 01:34:33,482
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1152
01:34:33,742 --> 01:34:34,482
[SPEAKER_02]: And it came out in the 40s?

1153
01:34:36,483 --> 01:34:38,784
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, it came out the year that we came here.

1154
01:34:39,124 --> 01:34:42,886
[SPEAKER_00]: As a matter of fact, I finished my part of it the night before I came to Cincinnati.

1155
01:34:42,926 --> 01:34:43,626
[SPEAKER_02]: Is that right?

1156
01:34:44,026 --> 01:34:44,987
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1157
01:34:45,067 --> 01:34:46,447
[SPEAKER_01]: Are you given proper credit?

1158
01:34:48,268 --> 01:34:49,728
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, not so much, no.

1159
01:34:49,789 --> 01:34:55,671
[SPEAKER_01]: I didn't bother, but it's verbatim in there from

1160
01:35:01,578 --> 01:35:04,360
[SPEAKER_01]: from the time I joined the Yankees until 1930.

1161
01:35:04,440 --> 01:35:08,723
[SPEAKER_01]: Is that right?

1162
01:35:12,346 --> 01:35:19,531
[SPEAKER_01]: And to tell you the truth, Frankie Graham was such a lovely fellow that he didn't owe me 10 cents.

1163
01:35:20,572 --> 01:35:26,296
[SPEAKER_01]: And you know that he saw that

1164
01:35:28,307 --> 01:35:34,734
[SPEAKER_01]: that they sent me commissions for over five years, I collected all that.

1165
01:35:42,118 --> 01:35:47,341
[SPEAKER_01]: But he, I knew Frankie since you see, I knew Frankie Graham since I was a kid.

1166
01:35:49,082 --> 01:35:51,823
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's how, and also Tom Meaney.

1167
01:35:51,883 --> 01:35:53,584
[SPEAKER_01]: I was going to ask you about Tom Meaney.

1168
01:35:53,744 --> 01:35:59,947
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, and all those folks, Tommy Holmes, John Drebbinger, John Drebbinger, and Daniel, of course.

1169
01:36:00,007 --> 01:36:10,493
[SPEAKER_01]: And then Daniel and Bill McGeehan and, what's his name, a great friend of mine who went to the

1170
01:36:11,098 --> 01:36:13,439
[SPEAKER_01]: became one of the officials of the Giants.

1171
01:36:13,499 --> 01:36:14,219
[SPEAKER_01]: Schumacher?

1172
01:36:14,399 --> 01:36:15,059
[SPEAKER_01]: Or Patterson?

1173
01:36:15,079 --> 01:36:18,781
[SPEAKER_01]: Gary Schumacher.

1174
01:36:23,883 --> 01:36:24,883
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I knew them all.

1175
01:36:25,583 --> 01:36:28,724
[SPEAKER_01]: I had good relations with them.

1176
01:36:28,845 --> 01:36:34,327
[SPEAKER_01]: I want to tell you something while I'm speaking of the press of those days.

1177
01:36:35,627 --> 01:36:38,248
[SPEAKER_01]: They were doing prohibition those days.

1178
01:36:39,136 --> 01:36:59,155
[SPEAKER_01]: It wasn't unusual for the phone to ring after practice or after a game and have somebody call up and say, hey, you got anything to drink up there?

1179
01:36:59,555 --> 01:37:01,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And it would be one of the newspaper men.

1180
01:37:02,157 --> 01:37:04,800
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'd say, no, come on up.

1181
01:37:05,778 --> 01:37:10,121
[SPEAKER_01]: And we'd sit around up there perhaps and talk.

1182
01:37:17,446 --> 01:37:23,250
[SPEAKER_01]: You know that never was there ever a confidence violated.

1183
01:37:23,270 --> 01:37:26,071
[SPEAKER_01]: They'd sit around and we'd talk with them.

1184
01:37:26,091 --> 01:37:27,192
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't do that today.

1185
01:37:27,252 --> 01:37:34,357
[SPEAKER_01]: I can remember one, I'm not going to mention any names, but one of our most prominent writers in New York, I was on a plane with him.

1186
01:37:35,139 --> 01:37:39,544
[SPEAKER_01]: going from New York to Detroit a few years ago.

1187
01:37:40,985 --> 01:37:43,948
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was in an intimate conversation with him.

1188
01:37:44,849 --> 01:37:50,655
[SPEAKER_01]: And we had a very nice time together.

1189
01:37:50,896 --> 01:37:53,358
[SPEAKER_01]: So I thought, until I read his

1190
01:37:56,686 --> 01:38:05,597
[SPEAKER_01]: until I read his column in one of the syndicated column in the paper, which he had distorted the whole, everything that I had said.

1191
01:38:06,799 --> 01:38:10,984
[SPEAKER_01]: And he's one of my favorite hates today.

1192
01:38:17,633 --> 01:38:28,238
[SPEAKER_01]: I can't understand these kind of fellows, and then a couple of fellows that are always criticizing the old-timers committee that want to know why so-and-so was elected.

1193
01:38:28,738 --> 01:38:31,079
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, they do strange things.

1194
01:38:33,860 --> 01:38:40,723
[SPEAKER_01]: They rushed in after we elected George Kelly of the Giants as the first baseman, and they

1195
01:38:44,049 --> 01:38:47,410
[SPEAKER_01]: wanted to know why we elected George Kelly.

1196
01:38:47,470 --> 01:39:01,554
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, George Kelly wasn't the greatest homerun hitter in the world, among a number of other things, and it seems today that everybody's guided by the homerun, you know.

1197
01:39:01,935 --> 01:39:10,597
[SPEAKER_01]: And I happen to say... Gee, this pen doesn't work very well.

1198
01:39:11,317 --> 01:39:12,378
[SPEAKER_01]: I happen to say that

1199
01:39:15,123 --> 01:39:23,907
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, he was a remarkable ball player and McGraw used him for things that you don't see nowadays.

1200
01:39:24,227 --> 01:39:39,934
[SPEAKER_01]: For instance, he was one of the fellas that went into the outfield behind second base and took the throws from the outfield as a relay to the home plate, which you don't see in the present day.

1201
01:39:40,575 --> 01:39:41,335
[SPEAKER_01]: You see what I mean?

1202
01:39:44,270 --> 01:40:01,495
[SPEAKER_01]: wrote the story, and the story appeared in the paper that he was elected, that I said that he was elected because he took the throws from the outfield and threw to the home plate.

1203
01:40:04,936 --> 01:40:11,358
[SPEAKER_01]: George Kelly was an instrument of defense that seconded none.

1204
01:40:14,920 --> 01:40:18,081
[SPEAKER_01]: And they just leave out that part.

1205
01:40:18,841 --> 01:40:22,643
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I can't tell you.

1206
01:40:23,483 --> 01:40:25,604
[SPEAKER_01]: There's been some enmity.

1207
01:40:28,505 --> 01:40:29,665
[SPEAKER_01]: Is Bob Roeg on there?

1208
01:40:29,865 --> 01:40:31,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

1209
01:40:31,906 --> 01:40:32,526
[SPEAKER_01]: Nice guy.

1210
01:40:33,867 --> 01:40:42,649
[SPEAKER_01]: But there's been some enmity between the regular writers and ourselves on that stuff, the old-timers committee.

1211
01:40:46,712 --> 01:40:48,693
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, those fellas just don't understand.

1212
01:40:49,193 --> 01:41:07,996
[SPEAKER_02]: Do you think there's a difference in writers today than in the past, as far as, well, you mentioned the one not involved in Congress, but in the reporting of the game, in their knowledge of the game, do they know baseball the way they used to?

1213
01:41:08,396 --> 01:41:08,836
[SPEAKER_02]: No, no.

1214
01:41:09,856 --> 01:41:12,977
[SPEAKER_02]: They go to the fifth inning and leave at the seventh, I understand, sometimes.

1215
01:41:13,996 --> 01:41:17,078
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't write the game stories much.

1216
01:41:17,138 --> 01:41:20,159
[SPEAKER_02]: I mean, you can't read an account of a game and find out what happened.

1217
01:41:21,220 --> 01:41:24,242
[SPEAKER_02]: You read what the interview is in the locker room after the game.

1218
01:41:25,462 --> 01:41:30,665
[SPEAKER_02]: The old-time games, the game accounts were exciting stories to read.

1219
01:41:32,146 --> 01:41:34,587
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I tell you, we must be fair.

1220
01:41:41,180 --> 01:42:06,712
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, they're having problems today, I imagine, because there are so many other sports that have jumped into prominence and have elevated themselves to the same plateau as baseball.

1221
01:42:06,772 --> 01:42:09,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And they are asked to cover these sports

1222
01:42:13,858 --> 01:42:18,620
[SPEAKER_02]: They're the products of a generation in which baseball is not the number one sport.

1223
01:42:18,680 --> 01:42:19,420
[SPEAKER_02]: That's right.

1224
01:42:19,500 --> 01:42:24,781
[SPEAKER_01]: It's part of the reflection.

1225
01:42:24,801 --> 01:42:29,383
[SPEAKER_02]: They don't have an interest or concern in it now.

1226
01:42:32,423 --> 01:42:40,466
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, don't you think that everybody today, including writers,

1227
01:42:53,861 --> 01:43:15,909
[SPEAKER_00]: are seeking, I won't say the easy way out, but they're filled with, not trickery, but I don't know what it is, some kind of a get the job done, this is good enough, you know, one of those kind of things.

1228
01:43:15,929 --> 01:43:16,989
[SPEAKER_00]: That's the way I feel.

1229
01:43:18,238 --> 01:43:21,300
[SPEAKER_00]: I mean, some of the ballplayers are the same way.

1230
01:43:22,541 --> 01:43:31,486
[SPEAKER_01]: I guess humanity's all the same, and I guess you and I are the same, too.

1231
01:43:32,506 --> 01:43:35,788
[SPEAKER_01]: We don't happen to think of it, though.

1232
01:43:36,289 --> 01:43:43,433
[SPEAKER_01]: Perhaps we're just as guilty of what we're criticizing others for as ourselves.

1233
01:43:48,360 --> 01:43:50,501
[SPEAKER_01]: But I always did like the press.

1234
01:43:50,561 --> 01:43:59,044
[SPEAKER_01]: The press always was good with me.

1235
01:44:09,538 --> 01:44:16,002
[SPEAKER_02]: I have a question, you're probably sick and tired of hearing it, but it's a kind of traditional one about the biggest thrill.

1236
01:44:16,282 --> 01:44:17,943
[SPEAKER_02]: Does anything stand out at all?

1237
01:44:18,323 --> 01:44:20,244
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, there were four thrills, really.

1238
01:44:20,824 --> 01:44:22,926
[SPEAKER_01]: This is what I write to people.

1239
01:44:28,049 --> 01:44:31,711
[SPEAKER_01]: The first one was, of course, signing when I was 15 years old.

1240
01:44:31,731 --> 01:44:35,953
[SPEAKER_01]: There could be nothing, because you must understand, Gene, that

1241
01:44:38,027 --> 01:44:39,368
[SPEAKER_01]: This is Governor Rhodes, you know.

1242
01:44:42,031 --> 01:44:46,254
[SPEAKER_01]: And that in those days, a kid was seen and not heard.

1243
01:44:46,775 --> 01:44:49,137
[SPEAKER_01]: You see, he didn't have the free license to speak.

1244
01:44:49,177 --> 01:44:55,022
[SPEAKER_00]: And his father used to say, when we want to hear from you, we'll ask you.

1245
01:44:55,603 --> 01:44:57,064
[SPEAKER_00]: And that kind of thing.

1246
01:44:57,204 --> 01:44:59,206
[SPEAKER_01]: And children are seen and not heard.

1247
01:45:07,451 --> 01:45:28,110
[SPEAKER_01]: And so consequently, when I signed when I was 15 years old, that became something of a national sensation, you see, because a kid

1248
01:45:32,343 --> 01:45:37,228
[SPEAKER_01]: signed at the age of 15, and that was, gee, practically unheard of.

1249
01:45:37,308 --> 01:45:41,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Kids had to be in early at night, and they weren't allowed here and there and that.

1250
01:45:41,792 --> 01:45:54,683
[SPEAKER_01]: And the second one was the first game against Detroit, and beating them 2 to 1 in 12 innings, and had an incident with Cobb and all that sort of thing.

1251
01:45:55,244 --> 01:45:56,625
[SPEAKER_01]: And you must recall, too,

1252
01:46:00,234 --> 01:46:16,225
[SPEAKER_01]: that I didn't have the money to wire my father or telephone or didn't know enough to send a telegram or anything of that nature.

1253
01:46:20,608 --> 01:46:21,249
[SPEAKER_01]: And I didn't

1254
01:46:24,757 --> 01:46:38,764
[SPEAKER_01]: I just walked on air, and he, the poor man at home, he could see that I had started the game, but he didn't know how it turned out, because he only got it about on the first page when they used to have those line scores, you know.

1255
01:46:39,345 --> 01:46:40,245
[SPEAKER_01]: When did he find out?

1256
01:46:40,465 --> 01:46:48,490
[SPEAKER_01]: He had a call with the New York Journal later that evening to find out that I'd won the game, see what I mean?

1257
01:46:48,810 --> 01:46:50,691
[SPEAKER_01]: But that was a big thrill.

1258
01:46:54,158 --> 01:46:55,099
[SPEAKER_01]: The 21 World Series?

1259
01:46:56,080 --> 01:46:57,241
[SPEAKER_02]: Lit.

1260
01:46:57,301 --> 01:46:59,863
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, and then the next one was the 21 World Series.

1261
01:46:59,883 --> 01:47:02,445
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, of course, that was the Supreme.

1262
01:47:03,226 --> 01:47:06,109
[SPEAKER_02]: What was the story in that last game, the one you lost again?

1263
01:47:06,649 --> 01:47:08,291
[SPEAKER_02]: Or was that the last game?

1264
01:47:08,311 --> 01:47:12,574
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they got a single and I walked.

1265
01:47:12,895 --> 01:47:18,900
[SPEAKER_01]: I thought I had, I think it was Ross Young struck out, but he took a base on balls that put two on and two out.

1266
01:47:20,056 --> 01:47:24,317
[SPEAKER_01]: And then somebody hit a ball to Peckinpah and the ball went through Peck's legs.

1267
01:47:29,178 --> 01:47:31,438
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the Hall of Fame was the next one.

1268
01:47:32,638 --> 01:47:34,799
[SPEAKER_02]: I can't make up my mind.

1269
01:47:48,619 --> 01:47:57,103
[SPEAKER_01]: Which was the more exciting moment, the actual induction or the moment I was notified?

1270
01:47:57,183 --> 01:48:05,807
[SPEAKER_00]: Because the moment I was notified was coming off the 18th tee.

1271
01:48:05,827 --> 01:48:12,210
[SPEAKER_01]: I suspect that probably was it.

1272
01:48:15,395 --> 01:48:19,119
[SPEAKER_01]: at the Belair Country Club down there, the W. Biltmore.

1273
01:48:19,559 --> 01:48:33,392
[SPEAKER_01]: And the caddy man said, hey, wait, you're wanted on long-distance phone.

1274
01:48:34,373 --> 01:48:38,036
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, who's calling?

1275
01:48:38,056 --> 01:48:39,377
[SPEAKER_01]: He says, Dayton, Ohio.

1276
01:48:41,023 --> 01:48:41,823
[SPEAKER_00]: Dayton, Ohio?

1277
01:48:41,883 --> 01:48:43,184
[SPEAKER_00]: Who the hell in Dayton, Ohio?

1278
01:48:43,204 --> 01:48:44,305
[SPEAKER_00]: What do they want me for?

1279
01:48:49,448 --> 01:48:53,310
[SPEAKER_01]: And I answered the phone.

1280
01:48:53,330 --> 01:48:54,390
[SPEAKER_01]: It was Ritter Collette.

1281
01:48:55,091 --> 01:48:57,592
[SPEAKER_01]: And Ritter said, congratulations.

1282
01:48:57,672 --> 01:48:58,453
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, on what?

1283
01:48:58,653 --> 01:49:01,054
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, being elected to the Hall of Fame.

1284
01:49:01,114 --> 01:49:04,276
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, come on, Ritt, you're kidding.

1285
01:49:04,536 --> 01:49:05,437
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, no.

1286
01:49:05,777 --> 01:49:06,537
[SPEAKER_01]: He said, that's true.

1287
01:49:06,557 --> 01:49:07,398
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, oh, no.

1288
01:49:08,772 --> 01:49:12,055
[SPEAKER_01]: RIT, please, please don't start.

1289
01:49:12,155 --> 01:49:12,676
[SPEAKER_01]: What do you want?"

1290
01:49:12,716 --> 01:49:20,223
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, please don't start any practical jokes because this is not a good time for practical jokes.

1291
01:49:21,784 --> 01:49:23,546
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, especially about a thing like that.

1292
01:49:23,606 --> 01:49:27,329
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, no, I'm not kidding you.

1293
01:49:29,206 --> 01:49:35,328
[SPEAKER_01]: Now all of a sudden I realized it was true and it broke me up.

1294
01:49:36,668 --> 01:49:38,709
[SPEAKER_01]: I couldn't talk to her no longer.

1295
01:49:38,789 --> 01:49:41,910
[SPEAKER_01]: I started to quiver and started to cry.

1296
01:49:43,190 --> 01:49:46,691
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, Rit, I'll have to call you back in about an hour.

1297
01:49:49,579 --> 01:49:52,680
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'll answer all the questions you want then.

1298
01:49:54,040 --> 01:49:57,081
[SPEAKER_01]: But I said, I can't talk to you any longer now.

1299
01:49:57,181 --> 01:49:58,822
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, I really can't.

1300
01:49:59,622 --> 01:50:01,102
[SPEAKER_01]: And I could hardly talk.

1301
01:50:01,162 --> 01:50:03,223
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm talking to you better than I talk to him.

1302
01:50:04,063 --> 01:50:09,565
[SPEAKER_01]: And the two, well, I have about five pros down at this place.

1303
01:50:10,265 --> 01:50:16,707
[SPEAKER_01]: One of them went in the next room in the bar in the cocktail lounge and made this announcement.

1304
01:50:16,727 --> 01:50:17,907
[SPEAKER_01]: And when I walked in,

1305
01:50:18,954 --> 01:50:25,016
[SPEAKER_01]: all stood up and gave me a standing ovation.

1306
01:50:25,836 --> 01:50:30,397
[SPEAKER_01]: And of course, I guess that's pretty hard to beat that moment.

1307
01:50:30,417 --> 01:50:33,678
[SPEAKER_01]: That was the first realization that I was in the Hall of Fame.

1308
01:50:34,478 --> 01:50:45,141
[SPEAKER_01]: And although the actual induction was a complete thrill in itself and something that can never occur again, nevertheless,

1309
01:50:47,646 --> 01:50:53,010
[SPEAKER_00]: Nevertheless, it was a wonderful thing.

1310
01:50:53,030 --> 01:50:59,196
[SPEAKER_00]: So what anybody asks me, I have a little difficult time telling them which is which.

1311
01:51:01,317 --> 01:51:07,582
[SPEAKER_01]: I've had a pretty complete life insofar as that type of thing.

1312
01:51:08,403 --> 01:51:10,905
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, I forgot I was on the air.

1313
01:51:10,945 --> 01:51:12,266
[SPEAKER_01]: Did you turn it off in time?

1314
01:51:13,888 --> 01:51:14,228
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

1315
01:51:14,348 --> 01:51:15,029
[SPEAKER_01]: Wait a minute.

1316
01:51:16,955 --> 01:51:19,477
[SPEAKER_02]: The Saturday Post article I asked him about.

1317
01:51:19,557 --> 01:51:19,877
[SPEAKER_01]: All right.

1318
01:51:20,858 --> 01:51:24,000
[SPEAKER_01]: He came to me in Montclair with the finished article.

1319
01:51:24,020 --> 01:51:24,701
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand?

1320
01:51:26,082 --> 01:51:31,086
[SPEAKER_01]: I told him, Stanley, that cannot go in the magazine the way it is.

1321
01:51:31,526 --> 01:51:34,588
[SPEAKER_01]: The last 600 to 800 words, you'll have to revise.

1322
01:51:35,109 --> 01:51:38,651
[SPEAKER_01]: Because this is knocking the National League, and I'm playing in the National League.

1323
01:51:39,032 --> 01:51:42,814
[SPEAKER_01]: There was a day I was supposed to be heading for spring training with the Brooklyn Dodgers.

1324
01:51:43,415 --> 01:51:45,576
[SPEAKER_01]: And I said, you can't print it that way, Stanley.

1325
01:51:46,681 --> 01:51:48,122
[SPEAKER_01]: And I don't want it that way.

1326
01:51:48,222 --> 01:51:50,743
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm very, very bitter about this.

1327
01:51:50,783 --> 01:51:54,885
[SPEAKER_01]: This is one of the most bitter things in my baseball history.

1328
01:51:55,605 --> 01:51:57,146
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, all right, I'll change that.

1329
01:51:57,166 --> 01:52:01,047
[SPEAKER_01]: I said, well, you've got to sweeten it because I don't believe that's the way you've written it at all.

1330
01:52:01,708 --> 01:52:04,049
[SPEAKER_01]: And by George, he never changed a word of it.

1331
01:52:04,229 --> 01:52:05,189
[SPEAKER_01]: Not one word of it.

1332
01:52:05,509 --> 01:52:10,892
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, Tom Meany had written a piece called Why the American League Wins.

1333
01:52:10,972 --> 01:52:13,853
[SPEAKER_01]: No, he wrote a piece called

1334
01:52:14,933 --> 01:52:18,080
[SPEAKER_01]: The new minor league, the national.

1335
01:52:19,558 --> 01:52:21,719
[SPEAKER_01]: That appeared the week before mine.

1336
01:52:21,759 --> 01:52:24,120
[SPEAKER_01]: Mine was called, Why the American League Wins.

1337
01:52:24,580 --> 01:52:34,285
[SPEAKER_01]: My piece was designed to just the same as you would talk to me about the difference between the bridge system of Sims and Culbertson.

1338
01:52:34,585 --> 01:52:35,285
[SPEAKER_01]: You understand?

1339
01:52:35,726 --> 01:52:43,449
[SPEAKER_01]: And I was talking to him or tried to talk to him about the difference between the systems of the American and National League.

1340
01:52:43,770 --> 01:52:48,712
[SPEAKER_01]: For instance, the National League pitched low because they wanted you to hit the ball on the ground for the double play.

1341
01:52:49,532 --> 01:52:58,895
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember the story of the article, the piece that I said that you pitch load Ruth and Gehrig and they hit home runs off you.

1342
01:52:58,935 --> 01:52:59,955
[SPEAKER_01]: This is nuts.

1343
01:53:00,655 --> 01:53:03,936
[SPEAKER_01]: And I'm giving you a quick example.

1344
01:53:03,976 --> 01:53:12,919
[SPEAKER_01]: Sid Keener or somebody who wrote a book or something and it was almost devoted to me, I guess, and told what a clown I was.

1345
01:53:14,537 --> 01:53:24,803
[SPEAKER_01]: to talk like that, that I was just offering some stupid suggestion and ridiculed me all through the book, see.

1346
01:53:25,023 --> 01:53:29,385
[SPEAKER_01]: And because Stanley Frank let the thing go, let the piece ride.

1347
01:53:29,405 --> 01:53:31,886
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I never said those things that he wrote.

1348
01:53:33,928 --> 01:53:40,252
[SPEAKER_01]: what I did say were qualified and were of a different connotation than he implied, you see.

1349
01:53:40,272 --> 01:53:41,893
[SPEAKER_01]: And where do I wind up?

1350
01:53:42,193 --> 01:53:56,263
[SPEAKER_01]: When I went to pitch in St. Louis, they wrote a piece in the St. Louis, some guy wrote a piece in the St. Louis paper said, Wade Hoyt, the guy who called the National League the Minor League, that was Meany's piece, is going to pitch today.

1351
01:53:57,024 --> 01:53:57,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Huh?

1352
01:53:57,384 --> 01:53:57,964
[SPEAKER_01]: Frank's piece?

1353
